nt="true"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span>
<div class="os-figure" id="CNX_UPhysics_06_02_PushedBox"><figure data-id="CNX_UPhysics_06_02_PushedBox" class="ui-has-child-figcaption">
<span data-type="media" id="fs-id1165037266385" data-alt="Here, may represent either the static or the kinetic frictional force. (a) An illustration of a man pushing a crate on a horizontal floor, exerting a force P directed horizontally to the right. (b) A free body diagram of the crate showing force P directed horizontally to the right, force f directed horizontally to the left, force N directed vertically up, and force w directed vertically down. An x y coordinate system is shown with positive x to the right and positive y up.">
<button type="button" aria-label="Click to enlarge image of Here, may represent either the static or the kinetic frictional force. (a) An illustration of a man pushing a crate on a horizontal floor, exerting a force P directed horizontally to the right. (b) A free body diagram of the crate showing force P directed horizontally to the right, force f directed horizontally to the left, force N directed vertically up, and force w directed vertically down. An x y coordinate system is shown with positive x to the right and positive y up." class="image-button-wrapper"><img src="/apps/image-cdn/v1/f=webp/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/2298ad9f42c8ef1c3290f61caa355ea1c196e80d" data-media-type="image/jpeg" alt="Here, may represent either the static or the kinetic frictional force. (a) An illustration of a man pushing a crate on a horizontal floor, exerting a force P directed horizontally to the right. (b) A free body diagram of the crate showing force P directed horizontally to the right, force f directed horizontally to the left, force N directed vertically up, and force w directed vertically down. An x y coordinate system is shown with positive x to the right and positive y up." width="880" height="374" loading="lazy" data-original-src="/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/2298ad9f42c8ef1c3290f61caa355ea1c196e80d"></button>
</span>

<figcaption class="os-caption-container">
  <span class="os-title-label">Figure </span>
<span class="os-number">6.13</span>



  <span class="os-divider"> </span>
  <span class="os-caption">(a) A crate on a horizontal surface is pushed with a force <math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle mathvariant="bold" mathsize="normal"><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><mstyle mathvariant="bold" mathsize="normal"><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>.</mo></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math> (b) The forces on the crate. Here, <math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mstyle mathvariant="bold" mathsize="normal"><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mstyle mathvariant="bold" mathsize="normal"><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mstyle></annotation-xml></semantics></math> may represent either the static or the kinetic frictional force.</span>
</figcaption></figure></div>
<p id="fs-id1165037206126"></p><h4 data-type="title">Strategy</h4>
The free-body diagram of the crate is shown in <a href="6-2-friction#CNX_UPhysics_06_02_PushedBox" class="autogenerated-content" aria-label="View Figure 6.13">Figure 6.13</a>(b). We apply Newton’s second law in the horizontal and vertical directions, including the friction force in opposition to the direction of motion of the box.
<p id="fs-id1165037984171"></p><h4 data-type="title">Solution</h4>Newton’s second law GIVES<div data-type="equation" id="fs-id1165038270337" class="unnumbered" data-label=""><math display="block"><semantics><mrow><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="left"><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd columnalign="left"><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="left"><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd columnalign="left"><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="left"><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd columnalign="left"><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="left"><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd columnalign="left"><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></annotation-xml></semantics></math></div>
<p id="fs-id1165036762837">Here we are using the symbol <em data-effect="italics">f</em> to represent the frictional force since we have not yet determined whether the crate is subject to station friction or kinetic friction. We do this whenever we are unsure what type of friction is acting. Now the weight of the crate is</p>
<div data-type="equation" id="fs-id1165038187209" class="unnumbered" data-label=""><math display="block"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>20.0</mn><mspace width="0.2em"></mspace><mtext>kg</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>9.80</mn><mspace width="0.2em"></mspace><msup><mrow><mtext>m/s</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>196</mn><mspace width="0.2em"></mspace><mtext>N,</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>20.0</mn><mspace width="0.2em"></mspace><mtext>kg</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>9.80</mn><mspace width="0.2em"></mspace><msup><mrow><mtext>m/s</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>196</mn><mspace width="0.2em"></mspace><mtext>N,</mtext></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></div>
<p id="fs-id1165038386213">which is also equal to <em data-effect="italics">N</em>. The maximum force of static friction is therefore <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>0.700</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>196</mn><mspace width="0.2em"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>137</mn><mspace width="0.2em"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>0.700</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>196</mn><mspace width="0.2em"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>137</mn><mspace width="0.2em"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> As long as <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mstyle mathvariant="bold" mathsize="normal"><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mstyle mathvariant="bold" mathsize="normal"><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mstyle></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> is less than 137 N, the force of static friction keeps the crate stationary and <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle mathvariant="bold" mathsize="normal"><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow